/-!
# A complete regular local ring is a power series ring

Let `(R, 𝔪)` be a Noetherian complete local ring which is regular and let
`d = dim_{R/𝔪} 𝔪/𝔪²` be its embedding dimension, which for a regular ring is the Krull dimension
of `R`. This file proves that `R` is a power series ring in `d` variables over a field in the two
cases where the residue field lifts to `R`: when `R` contains a field, and when a field `k` is
given together with a map `k → R` inducing an isomorphism onto `R/𝔪`.

The second case carries the argument. Pick `f_1, …, f_d ∈ 𝔪` generating `𝔪` — there are `d` of
them because `d` is the minimal number of generators of `𝔪` — and let
`Φ : k[[x_1, …, x_d]] → R` be the continuous `k`-algebra map with `Φ(x_i) = f_i`. It is surjective
because it is surjective modulo the ideal generated by the variables and both sides are complete
for that ideal. It is injective because a nonzero `r` in its kernel would give
`dim R = dim k[[x]]/ker Φ ≤ dim k[[x]]/(r) < dim k[[x]] ≤ d`, whereas `dim R = d` by regularity.
That last step needs nothing about power series: a surjection onto a ring of at least the same
Krull dimension, out of a ring without zero divisors of at most that dimension, is injective.

The first case reduces to the second: by the Cohen structure theorem `R` has a coefficient ring
`Λ`, whose maximal ideal is generated by the characteristic `p` of the residue field; a field
inside `R` forces `p` to vanish in `R`, so `Λ` is a field, and it maps isomorphically onto the
residue field, which therefore lifts to `R` — a coefficient field, splitting `R → R/𝔪`.

## Main results

* `Stacks.CompleteLocalRing.PowerSeriesRing.solution`: the proof of [StacksProject 0C0S].

## Implementation notes

The power series facts the dimension count needs are in Mathlib only in one variable, so they are
proved here or in `StacksFrontier.RingTheory.MvPowerSeries.RegularLocal`. The ideal generated by the
variables in `k[[x_1, …, x_n]]` is the kernel of the constant coefficient
(`MvPowerSeries.span_X_eq_ker_constantCoeff`, by induction on the number of variables through
`MvPowerSeries.finSuccEquiv`), the one-variable case being `PowerSeries.maximalIdeal_eq_span_X`.
Over a field that kernel is the maximal ideal, so the maximal ideal is generated by the `n`
variables and the Krull dimension is at most `n`.

The dimension count itself, `Stacks.CompleteLocalRing.PowerSeriesRing.injective_of_ringKrullDim_le`,
mentions no power series: a surjection out of a ring without zero divisors of Krull dimension at
most `n`, onto a ring of Krull dimension at least `n`, is injective, because killing a nonzero
element of the source drops its dimension and leaves no room for the target.

A finitely generated submodule is spanned by the range of a family indexed by `Fin p.spanFinrank`
(`Stacks.CompleteLocalRing.PowerSeriesRing.exists_fin_generating_family`); Mathlib's
`Submodule.fg_iff_exists_fin_generating_family` leaves the index count unspecified and
`Submodule.FG.exists_span_finset_card_eq_spanFinrank` produces a `Finset`. It is applied with the
side condition `spanFinrank 𝔪 = d` discharged in place, so that `d` itself indexes the generators.

The surjectivity of evaluation at a generating family of `I` on an `I`-adically complete ring —
Stacks 0315 — is the same argument as in
`StacksFrontier/RingTheory/CompleteLocalRing/CohenStructureTheorem.lean`, where it is the private
lemma of the same name; a proof file cannot be imported, so it is repeated here.

## References

* [StacksProject 0C0S](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0C0S), Lemma 10.160.10
* [StacksProject 032A](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/032A), Theorem 10.160.8
* [StacksProject 0315](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0315), Lemma 10.96.1
-/

namespace Stacks.CompleteLocalRing.PowerSeriesRing

open IsLocalRing MvPowerSeries

/-- The maximal ideal of a power series ring in finitely many variables over a field is generated
by the variables. This is the multivariate `PowerSeries.maximalIdeal_eq_span_X`. -/
private theorem maximalIdeal_eq_span_range_X (k : Type*) [Field k] (n : ℕ) :
    maximalIdeal (MvPowerSeries (Fin n) k) =
      Ideal.span (Set.range (X : Fin n → MvPowerSeries (Fin n) k)) := by
  rw [MvPowerSeries.span_X_eq_ker_constantCoeff,
    IsLocalRing.ker_eq_maximalIdeal (constantCoeff : MvPowerSeries (Fin n) k →+* k)
      fun c => ⟨C c, by simp⟩]

/-- The Krull dimension of a power series ring in `n` variables over a field is at most `n`. -/
@[ega "0 17.1.4" "(iii)"]
private theorem ringKrullDim_mvPowerSeries_le (k : Type*) [Field k] (n : ℕ) :
    ringKrullDim (MvPowerSeries (Fin n) k) ≤ (n : WithBot ℕ∞) := by
  have h : (maximalIdeal (MvPowerSeries (Fin n) k)).spanFinrank ≤ n := by
    rw [maximalIdeal_eq_span_range_X]
    refine (Submodule.spanFinrank_span_le_ncard_of_finite (Set.finite_range _)).trans ?_
    simpa [← Set.image_univ] using
      Set.ncard_image_le (f := (X : Fin n → MvPowerSeries (Fin n) k)) Set.finite_univ
  exact (ringKrullDim_le_spanFinrank_maximalIdeal _).trans (by exact_mod_cast h)

/-- A surjection out of a ring without zero divisors onto a ring of at least the same Krull
dimension is injective, provided the source has dimension at most that: a nonzero element of the
kernel would cut the dimension of the source strictly, leaving no room for the target. -/
private theorem injective_of_ringKrullDim_le {A B : Type*} [CommRing A] [NoZeroDivisors A]
    [CommRing B] {n : ℕ} (F : A →+* B) (hF : Function.Surjective F)
    (hA : ringKrullDim A ≤ (n : WithBot ℕ∞)) (hB : (n : WithBot ℕ∞) ≤ ringKrullDim B) :
    Function.Injective F := by
  rw [injective_iff_map_eq_zero]
  intro r hr
  by_contra hr0
  have hle : Ideal.span {r} ≤ RingHom.ker F := Ideal.span_le.2 (by simpa using hr)
  have hquot : (n : WithBot ℕ∞) ≤ ringKrullDim (A ⧸ Ideal.span {r}) := by
    refine hB.trans ?_
    rw [← ringKrullDim_eq_of_ringEquiv (RingHom.quotientKerEquivOfSurjective hF)]
    exact ringKrullDim_le_of_surjective (Ideal.Quotient.factor hle)
      (Ideal.Quotient.factor_surjective hle)
  have hcontra : ((n + 1 : ℕ) : WithBot ℕ∞) ≤ (n : WithBot ℕ∞) := by
    push_cast
    calc (n : WithBot ℕ∞) + 1 ≤ ringKrullDim (A ⧸ Ideal.span {r}) + 1 := by gcongr
      _ ≤ ringKrullDim A :=
          ringKrullDim_quotient_succ_le_of_nonZeroDivisor (mem_nonZeroDivisors_of_ne_zero hr0)
      _ ≤ (n : WithBot ℕ∞) := hA
  rw [Nat.cast_le] at hcontra
  omega

/-- A finitely generated submodule is spanned by the range of a family indexed by
`Fin p.spanFinrank`. Mathlib's `Submodule.fg_iff_exists_fin_generating_family` leaves the index
count unspecified and `Submodule.FG.exists_span_finset_card_eq_spanFinrank` produces a `Finset`. -/
theorem exists_fin_generating_family {R M : Type*} [Semiring R] [AddCommMonoid M]
    [Module R M] {p : Submodule R M} (hp : p.FG) {n : ℕ} (hn : p.spanFinrank = n) :
    ∃ y : Fin n → M, Submodule.span R (Set.range y) = p := by
  obtain ⟨s, hcard, hspan⟩ := hp.exists_span_finset_card_eq_spanFinrank
  let e : Fin n ≃ {x // x ∈ s} := (finCongr (hcard.trans hn).symm).trans s.equivFin.symm
  refine ⟨fun i => (e i : M), ?_⟩
  have hrange : Set.range (fun i => ((e i : M))) = (s : Set M) := by
    ext x
    refine ⟨fun ⟨i, hi⟩ => hi ▸ (e i).2, fun hx => ⟨e.symm ⟨x, hx⟩, by simp⟩⟩
  rw [hrange]
  exact hspan

/-- Part (2) of Stacks 0C0S, which is also the whole content of part (1): a complete regular
local ring `R` of embedding dimension `d` over a field `k` mapping isomorphically onto its
residue field is the power series ring `k[[x_1, …, x_d]]` as a `k`-algebra. -/
@[ega "0 19.6.4" "special case"]
private theorem nonempty_algEquiv_mvPowerSeries (R : Type u) [CommRing R] [IsRegularLocalRing R]
    [IsAdicComplete (maximalIdeal R) R] (d : ℕ)
    (hd : Module.finrank (ResidueField R) (CotangentSpace R) = d)
    (k : Type v) [Field k] [Algebra k R]
    (hk : Function.Bijective ((residue R).comp (algebraMap k R))) :
    Nonempty (R ≃ₐ[k] MvPowerSeries (Fin d) k) := by
  obtain ⟨y, hy⟩ := exists_fin_generating_family (IsNoetherian.noetherian (maximalIdeal R))
    (by rw [spanFinrank_maximalIdeal_eq_finrank_cotangentSpace, hd])
  obtain ⟨F, hFsurj, -, hFC⟩ :=
    Stacks.CompleteLocalRing.CohenStructure.exists_surjective_eval₂Hom (maximalIdeal R) y hy
      (algebraMap k R) hk.2
  have hinj : Function.Injective F :=
    injective_of_ringKrullDim_le F hFsurj (ringKrullDim_mvPowerSeries_le k d)
      (by rw [← hd]; exact ((IsRegularLocalRing.iff_finrank_cotangentSpace R).1 ‹_›).le)
  refine ⟨(AlgEquiv.ofRingEquiv (f := RingEquiv.ofBijective F ⟨hinj, hFsurj⟩) ?_).symm⟩
  intro c
  show F (algebraMap k (MvPowerSeries (Fin d) k) c) = algebraMap k R c
  simpa [MvPowerSeries.algebraMap_apply] using hFC c

/-- A complete local ring containing a field has a coefficient field: a ring map from its residue
field splitting the residue map. By the Cohen structure theorem the coefficient ring `Λ` of `R`
has maximal ideal generated by the residue characteristic, which vanishes in `R` because `R`
contains a field; so `Λ` is a field mapping isomorphically onto the residue field. -/
private theorem exists_ringHom_residue_comp_eq_id (R : Type u) [CommRing R] [IsLocalRing R]
    [IsAdicComplete (maximalIdeal R) R] (F : Type v) [Field F] [Algebra F R] :
    ∃ f : ResidueField R →+* R, (residue R).comp f = RingHom.id (ResidueField R) := by
  obtain ⟨Λ, hΛ⟩ :=
    (IsLocalRing.exists_isCoefficientRing_and_exists_surjective_mvPowerSeries R).1
  have := hΛ.isLocalHom
  -- the residue characteristic vanishes in `R`, because `R` contains a field
  let _ : Algebra F (ResidueField R) := ((residue R).comp (algebraMap F R)).toAlgebra
  have : CharP (ResidueField R) (ringChar F) :=
    charP_of_injective_algebraMap
      (RingHom.injective (algebraMap F (ResidueField R))) (ringChar F)
  have hchar : (ringChar (ResidueField R) : R) = 0 := by
    rw [CharP.eq (ResidueField R) (ringChar.charP (ResidueField R)) ‹_›,
      ← map_natCast (algebraMap F R) (ringChar F), ringChar.Nat.cast_ringChar, map_zero]
  -- hence the coefficient ring is a field
  have hbot : maximalIdeal Λ.toSubring = ⊥ := by
    rw [← IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal_comap Λ.toSubring.subtype,
      hΛ.comap_maximalIdeal_eq_span_ringChar, Ideal.span_singleton_eq_bot]
    exact Subtype.ext (by push_cast; exact hchar)
  let _ : Field Λ.toSubring := (isField_iff_maximalIdeal_eq.2 hbot).toField
  -- and it maps isomorphically onto the residue field of `R`, so its inverse splits `R → R/𝔪`
  have hbij : Function.Bijective ((residue R).comp Λ.toSubring.subtype) := by
    refine ⟨RingHom.injective _, fun y => ?_⟩
    obtain ⟨x, hx⟩ := hΛ.residueField_map_surjective y
    obtain ⟨z, hz⟩ : ∃ z, residue Λ.toSubring z = x := Ideal.Quotient.mk_surjective x
    exact ⟨z, by rw [RingHom.comp_apply, ← ResidueField.map_residue Λ.toSubring.subtype z, hz, hx]⟩
  exact ⟨Λ.toSubring.subtype.comp (RingEquiv.ofBijective _ hbij).symm.toRingHom,
    RingHom.ext fun x => (RingEquiv.ofBijective _ hbij).apply_symm_apply x⟩

/-- Part (1) of Stacks 0C0S: a complete regular local ring containing a field has a coefficient
field, namely its own residue field, so part (2) applies to it. -/
@[ega "0 19.6.4" "c ⇒ b' for complete A",
  ega "0 19.8.8" "(i), special case",
  sga "2 IV 5.5" "partial"]
private theorem nonempty_ringEquiv_mvPowerSeries (R : Type u) [CommRing R] [IsRegularLocalRing R]
    [IsAdicComplete (maximalIdeal R) R] (d : ℕ)
    (hd : Module.finrank (ResidueField R) (CotangentSpace R) = d)
    (F : Type v) [Field F] [Algebra F R] :
    Nonempty (R ≃+* MvPowerSeries (Fin d) (ResidueField R)) := by
  obtain ⟨f, hf⟩ := exists_ringHom_residue_comp_eq_id R F
  let _ : Algebra (ResidueField R) R := f.toAlgebra
  obtain ⟨φ⟩ := nonempty_algEquiv_mvPowerSeries R d hd (ResidueField R)
    (by rw [show (residue R).comp (algebraMap (ResidueField R) R) = RingHom.id _ from hf]
        exact Function.bijective_id)
  exact ⟨φ.toRingEquiv⟩

end Stacks.CompleteLocalRing.PowerSeriesRing

/-!
# A complete regular local ring is a power series ring

Let `(R, 𝔪)` be a Noetherian complete local ring which is regular, let `κ = R/𝔪` be its residue
field, and let `d = dim_κ 𝔪/𝔪²` be its embedding dimension, which for a regular ring is the
Krull dimension of `R`. Then `R` is a power series ring in `d` variables in the two cases where
the residue field lifts to `R`:

1. if `R` contains a field — equivalently, if `R` contains `𝐅_p` or `𝐐`, equivalently if `R` and
   `κ` have the same characteristic — then `R ≅ κ[[x_1, …, x_d]]` as rings;
2. if `k` is a field and `k → R` is a ring map inducing an isomorphism `k → κ`, then
   `R ≅ k[[x_1, …, x_d]]` as `k`-algebras.

The first case reduces to the second: by the Cohen structure theorem (Stacks 032A) `R` has a
coefficient ring, and when `R` contains a field that coefficient ring is a field mapping
isomorphically to `κ`. In the second case one picks `f_1, …, f_d ∈ 𝔪` lifting a basis of `𝔪/𝔪²`
and considers the continuous `k`-algebra map `k[[x_1, …, x_d]] → R` sending `x_i` to `f_i`. It is
surjective by Stacks 0315, both sides being `(x_1, …, x_d)`-adically complete and the map being
surjective modulo that ideal by Nakayama. It is injective because a kernel would make the
dimension of `R` smaller than `d` (Stacks 00KW), contradicting regularity.

## Main results

* `IsRegularLocalRing.nonempty_ringEquiv_and_algEquiv_mvPowerSeries`: a complete regular local
  ring containing a field is isomorphic to a power series ring over its residue field, and if a
  field `k` maps to it inducing an isomorphism onto the residue field then it is isomorphic to a
  power series ring over `k` as a `k`-algebra; in both cases the number of variables is the
  embedding dimension.
* `MvPowerSeries.maximalIdeal_eq_span_range_X`: the maximal ideal of `k[[x_1, …, x_n]]` is
  generated by the variables.
* `MvPowerSeries.ringKrullDim_eq`: `k[[x_1, …, x_n]]` has Krull dimension `n`, with the two
  inequalities available separately as `MvPowerSeries.ringKrullDim_le` and
  `MvPowerSeries.natCast_le_ringKrullDim`.

## Implementation notes

The source's "Noetherian complete local ring, regular" is `[IsRegularLocalRing R]` together with
`[IsAdicComplete (maximalIdeal R) R]`: `IsRegularLocalRing` already contains local and Noetherian
and says `spanFinrank 𝔪 = dim R`, while `IsAdicComplete` is the source's `R ≅ lim_n R/𝔪ⁿ`, its
`IsHausdorff` half being `⋂ 𝔪ⁿ = 0` and its `IsPrecomplete` half the surjectivity.

The number of variables is pinned rather than left existential: `d` is the embedding dimension
`Module.finrank (ResidueField R) (CotangentSpace R)`, which is `Submodule.spanFinrank 𝔪` and
hence, `R` being regular, is `ringKrullDim R`. This is the `d` the source's own proof produces —
the rank of `𝔪/𝔪²` — so pinning it costs the proof nothing and it is what a reader of the
conclusion wants. It is taken as an explicit argument with the defining hypothesis `hd` so that
both conjuncts read in terms of `d`.

"`R` contains either `𝐅_p` or `𝐐`" is stated as: for every field `F` with a ring map `F → R`.
A ring map from a field to a nonzero ring is injective, so this hypothesis says exactly that `R`
contains a subfield, and a subfield of `R` contains its prime field, which is `𝐅_p` or `𝐐`;
conversely `𝐅_p` and `𝐐` are such an `F`. For a local ring the condition is also equivalent to
`ringChar R = ringChar (ResidueField R)`, equal characteristic. `F` does not appear in the
conclusion, because the source's isomorphism in this case is one of rings over the residue field
and not over `F`: the coefficient field furnished by Stacks 032A is a lift of `κ` and need not
contain a prescribed `F`.

"`R` is isomorphic to a power series ring" is `Nonempty (R ≃+* MvPowerSeries (Fin d) κ)`,
respectively `Nonempty (R ≃ₐ[k] MvPowerSeries (Fin d) k)`, with the `d` variables indexed by
`Fin d` as the source indexes `x_1, …, x_d`.

The source's two parts are the two conjuncts of one statement, each guarded by its own
hypothesis, exactly as in Stacks 032A, so that neither part is weakened by the other's
hypothesis.

Faithfulness. (1) Every hypothesis is used by the conclusion: `IsRegularLocalRing R` is what makes
`d` the Krull dimension and what makes the power series map injective — without regularity `R` is
only a quotient of a power series ring, which is Stacks 032A and not this statement;
`IsAdicComplete (maximalIdeal R) R` is what makes that map surjective; `hd` names `d`; and each
conjunct's own antecedent — a field inside `R`, respectively a field mapping isomorphically onto
`κ` — is what makes the coefficient ring a field, so that the power series are taken over a field
rather than over a Cohen ring. (2) The scalars and the base are the source's: in the second part
the isomorphism is `k`-algebraic for the given `Algebra k R`, the source's own `k → R`, not over
`κ` or over a completion of `k`, and in the first part the coefficients are `R`'s own residue
field `IsLocalRing.ResidueField R`. (3) The one map the source names is the map `k → R/𝔪` of the
second part's hypothesis, and it is pinned as `(residue R).comp (algebraMap k R)`, the composite
of the given `k → R` with the residue map; the isomorphisms in the conclusions are only asserted
to exist, as in the source, because they depend on a choice of regular system of parameters
`f_1, …, f_d` and no choice is canonical. (4) The instantiation is not degenerate: `R = k⟦X⟧` for
a field `k` satisfies every hypothesis, with `d = 1` (its maximal ideal is `(X)`, principal and
nonzero) and with `F = k` in the first part and `k` itself in the second, the residue field being
`k` by `PowerSeries.residueFieldOfPowerSeries`; `d = 0` happens exactly when `R` is a field, where
both parts assert `R ≅ κ`.

